{{!-- The player's own settings, surfaced where they read their character rather than three
      menus deep in Foundry's sidebar. Every control here writes the same client setting the
      settings menu writes (module/utils/sheet-preferences.js) — nothing on this tab is stored
      on the actor, and nothing is this sheet's alone.

      Which is what the note at the top says, and it is not decoration: a control sitting on
      Corvus's sheet reads as Corvus's, and it is not. These are per-PERSON, so a change here
      shows on every Stonetop sheet this browser opens, including the other characters of a
      player running two.

      ONE ROW IS NOT, and it announces itself: the Fight tab switch is world-scoped and GM-only,
      and carries a `.stonetop-preference-world` line saying it is the table's. Anything else
      that has to be told apart from the rows around it belongs in that same sentence, not in a
      colour a reader has to already know the meaning of.

      NO `name` ATTRIBUTES on any input below. A named input inside an actor sheet's form rides
      along into `_updateObject` as a stray top-level key — see the note in roll-mode-radios.hbs,
      which keeps its names only because radios need them for the browser's own grouping. These
      controls are checkboxes, selects and ranges, none of which group, so they are addressed by
      `data-pref` (the setting key) and the form never sees them.

      Labels, hints, choices and slider bounds all come from the setting REGISTRATION, so this
      template names no setting and carries no copy of a hint that could drift from the one the
      settings menu shows. --}}
<div class="tab preferences" data-group="primary" data-tab="preferences">
	<section class="sheet-tab stonetop-preferences">

		<p class="stonetop-preferences-intro">
			{{localize "stonetop.sheet.preferences.intro"}}
		</p>

		{{!-- The filter over everything below, and the one search on these sheets that is drawn
		      ALREADY OPEN (`searchPinned`). Every other one hides behind a magnifying glass beside
		      a heading, where the reader has arrived to look at a list and the box is a second
		      thought. This tab is the opposite: it is six folds of rows a reader reaches WANTING
		      one of them, usually the one that makes the text bigger, and a settings search that
		      has to be found before it can be used is a search the person who most needs it will
		      not find. It sits with the intro, inside the rule that divides this preamble from
		      the settings, so the sentence explaining whose settings these are and the way to
		      reach one read as the one block they are.

		      It matches a row's whole text — label, hint, the option labels inside a select — so
		      "serif" finds the font row and "reload" finds the Fight tab switch by its warning.
		      Wired in module/utils/preferences-tab.js; an active term also forces open any group
		      the reader had folded, so a match is never hiding inside a shut section. --}}
		<div class="stonetop-preferences-controls">
			{{> "stonetop.tab-search-control" searchPinned=true
			    title=(localize "stonetop.sheet.preferences.searchLabel")
			    placeholder=(localize "stonetop.sheet.preferences.searchPlaceholder")}}
		</div>

		{{!-- Shown by the filter when a term matches nothing. Without it the tab answers a typo
		      with the intro, a blank space and the Foundry settings button, which reads as a tab
		      that has broken rather than as a search that found nothing. --}}
		<p class="stonetop-preferences-no-matches" role="status">
			{{localize "stonetop.sheet.preferences.noMatches"}}
		</p>

		{{#each stonetop.preferences}}
		<div class="stonetop-preference-group" data-preference-group="{{id}}">
			{{> "stonetop.section-heading" title=title collapse=collapse}}

			{{#each rows}}
			<div class="stonetop-preference-row" data-preference-key="{{key}}">
				{{#if isCheck}}
				{{!-- The checkbox is INSIDE the label so the whole title line toggles it, which
				      is how every other checklist on these sheets behaves. `.stonetop-check`
				      takes the shared 15px glyph skin; core's own margin is left alone, which is
				      what seats the glyph on the first line of the label. --}}
				<label class="stonetop-preference-label" for="stonetop-pref-{{key}}">
					<input id="stonetop-pref-{{key}}" type="checkbox" class="stonetop-check"
					       data-pref="{{key}}"{{#if checked}} checked{{/if}}>
					<span>{{label}}</span>
				</label>
				{{else}}
				<label class="stonetop-preference-label" for="stonetop-pref-{{key}}">
					<span>{{label}}</span>
				</label>

				{{#if isChoice}}
				<select id="stonetop-pref-{{key}}" class="stonetop-preference-select" data-pref="{{key}}">
					{{#each choices}}
					<option value="{{value}}"{{#if selected}} selected{{/if}}>{{label}}</option>
					{{/each}}
				</select>
				{{/if}}

				{{#if isRange}}
				{{!-- The readout is repainted live by the sheet's `input` handler; the setting
				      itself is written on `change`. A dragged handle fires `input` on every
				      pixel and `change` once, when it is let go — so the number tracks the drag
				      and only the value landed on is stored, rather than a burst of localStorage
				      writes and onChange work for every value passed through on the way. (Arrow
				      keys fire both per press, which is one press to one stored value anyway.) --}}
				<div class="stonetop-preference-range">
					<input id="stonetop-pref-{{key}}" type="range" data-pref="{{key}}"
					       min="{{min}}" max="{{max}}" step="{{step}}" value="{{value}}">
					<output class="stonetop-preference-range-value"
					        for="stonetop-pref-{{key}}">{{display}}</output>
				</div>
				{{/if}}
				{{/if}}

				{{!-- No "something else is overriding this" note, and no `disabled` on the controls
				      above: no row on this tab can be made dead by another row. One used to be
				      ("Paper Texture" while Sheet Contrast was High) and the settings were
				      uncoupled instead, which is the fix a row like that wants. See the note at
				      the top of module/utils/sheet-preferences.js. --}}
				{{#if hint}}<p class="notes">{{hint}}</p>{{/if}}

				{{!-- Whose setting this is. The intro above promises that nothing on this tab
				      leaves this browser, and the one world-scoped row here breaks that promise
				      on purpose (the Fight tab is the table's, not the reader's) — so it says
				      so, on the row, rather than leaving the intro quietly wrong. The flag is
				      read off the setting's own registration, so a world row added later cannot
				      ship without this line. --}}
				{{#if isWorld}}
				<p class="notes stonetop-preference-world">
					<i class="fas fa-users" aria-hidden="true"></i>
					{{localize "stonetop.sheet.preferences.worldScoped"}}
				</p>
				{{/if}}
			</div>
			{{/each}}

			{{#if menu}}
			<div class="stonetop-preference-row stonetop-preference-menu-row">
				<button type="button" class="stonetop-preference-menu" data-preference-menu="{{menu.id}}">
					<i class="fas fa-sliders" aria-hidden="true"></i>
					<span>{{menu.label}}</span>
				</button>
				{{#if menu.hint}}<p class="notes">{{menu.hint}}</p>{{/if}}
			</div>
			{{/if}}
		</div>
		{{/each}}

		{{!-- The escape hatch, and the reason the tab does not try to be the whole settings menu:
		      the world-wide options, the GM-only gates and everything a module registered are all
		      still over there, and a player who wants one should not have to be told where it
		      lives. Rendered for everyone — Foundry's settings sidebar is not GM-only, it simply
		      shows a player fewer rows. --}}
		<p class="stonetop-preferences-more">
			<button type="button" class="stonetop-preferences-open-settings">
				<i class="fas fa-gear" aria-hidden="true"></i>
				<span>{{localize "stonetop.sheet.preferences.openSettings"}}</span>
			</button>
		</p>

	</section>
</div>
